## Proof

Let $V = V(F_1,\dots,F_N) \subseteq \mathbb{P}^n$ with each $F_i$ homogeneous of degree $d_i$. Write $J(p) = \left(\dfrac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j}(p)\right)_{\substack{i=1,\dots,N\\j=0,\dots,n}}$ for the **Jacobian matrix** evaluated at $p$.

**Theorem (Jacobian criterion for projective algebraic sets).** *The singular locus of $V$ is*
$$\operatorname{Sing}(V) \;=\; \bigl\{\, p \in V \;:\; \operatorname{rank} J(p) \;<\; \operatorname{codim}(V) \;=\; n - \dim V \,\bigr\}.$$

*In particular, $p\in V$ is nonsingular iff $\operatorname{rank} J(p) = n - \dim V$.*

---

### Step 1. The affine Jacobian criterion

Work first in affine space $\mathbb{A}^n$ with coordinates $y_1,\dots,y_n$. For an affine variety $X = V(g_1,\dots,g_M)$ with ideal $I(X)=(g_1,\dots,g_M)$, the **embedded (Zariski) tangent space** at $p\in X$ is
$$T_p X \;=\; \bigl\{\, v \in \mathbb{A}^n \;:\; \textstyle\sum_{k} \frac{\partial g_i}{\partial y_k}(p)\, v_k = 0 \;\;\forall\, i \,\bigr\} \;=\; \ker J_{\text{aff}}(p).$$
Hence $\dim T_p X = \dim \ker J_{\text{aff}}(p) = n - \operatorname{rank} J_{\text{aff}}(p)$.

A point $p\in X$ is **nonsingular** (smooth) iff $\dim T_p X = \dim X$. Since always $\dim T_p X \ge \dim X$, nonsingularity is equivalent to
$$\operatorname{rank} J_{\text{aff}}(p) \;=\; n - \dim X \;=\; \operatorname{codim}(X).$$
This is the standard affine Jacobian criterion (see, e.g., Hartshorne I.5, or Shafarevich I.5). For it to give the correct codimension one uses the fact that locally at $p$, the ideal $I(X)$ is generated by exactly $\operatorname{codim}(X)$ independent equations; the rank of the full Jacobian of any generating set equals the codimension precisely at smooth points.

### Step 2. Passing to a projective affine chart

Cover $\mathbb{P}^n$ by the standard charts $U_\ell = \{x_\ell \neq 0\}$, identified with $\mathbb{A}^n$ via $y_j = x_j/x_\ell$ ($j\neq \ell$). For $p\in V\cap U_\ell$, dehomogenize:
$$f_i^{(\ell)}(y) \;=\; y_\ell^{-d_i}\, F_i\bigl(\dots,y_j,\dots\bigr), \qquad y_\ell = 1.$$
Then $V\cap U_\ell = V(f_1^{(\ell)},\dots,f_N^{(\ell)})\subset \mathbb{A}^n$, and $p$ is singular in $V$ iff $p$ is singular in the affine variety $V\cap U_\ell$.

### Step 3. Relating the affine and projective Jacobians

By the chain rule, the affine Jacobian $J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p)$ (an $N\times n$ matrix in the $n$ variables $\{y_j : j\neq \ell\}$) is obtained from the projective Jacobian $J(p)$ (an $N\times(n+1)$ matrix) by deleting the $\ell$-th column and rescaling columns by $x_\ell$ (a harmless invertible operation on charts). Concretely,
$$\frac{\partial f_i^{(\ell)}}{\partial y_j}(p) \;=\; \frac{1}{x_\ell(p)^{d_i}}\left(\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j}(p) - \frac{x_j(p)}{x_\ell(p)}\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_\ell}(p)\right), \qquad j\neq \ell.$$
Thus the columns of $J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p)$ are the columns of $J(p)$ (excluding column $\ell$) each **projected off** the $\ell$-th column. Equivalently, the row space of $J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p)$ is the image of the row space of $J(p)$ under projection along the $\ell$-th coordinate, so
$$\operatorname{rank} J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p) \;\ge\; \operatorname{rank} J(p) - 1,$$
with equality unless the $\ell$-th column of $J(p)$ is a linear combination of the others modulo the row relations — a degeneracy we now rule out on $V$.

### Step 4. Euler's relation kills the radial direction

Since $F_i$ is homogeneous of degree $d_i$, Euler's identity gives
$$\sum_{j=0}^{n} x_j\,\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j} \;=\; d_i\, F_i.$$
Evaluating at $p\in V$ (so $F_i(p)=0$):
$$\sum_{j=0}^{n} x_j(p)\,\frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j}(p) \;=\; 0 \qquad \forall\, i.$$
In matrix form, $J(p)\cdot \mathbf{x}(p)^{\!\top} = 0$. Hence the **projective coordinate vector** $\mathbf{x}(p)=(x_0(p),\dots,x_n(p))\neq 0$ lies in $\ker J(p)$. This vector is exactly the "radial" direction of the line $\{\lambda\mathbf{x}(p)\}$ through $p$ — the direction that projectivization quotients out.

Consequently, on any chart $U_\ell\ni p$ (where $x_\ell(p)\neq 0$), the $\ell$-th column of $J(p)$ is a linear combination of the remaining columns *within* $\ker$-relations, and the projection in Step 3 loses **exactly** this one radial direction:
$$\operatorname{rank} J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p) \;=\; \operatorname{rank} J(p) - 1.$$
Therefore
$$\dim T_p(V\cap U_\ell) \;=\; n - \operatorname{rank} J_{\text{aff}}^{(\ell)}(p) \;=\; n - \bigl(\operatorname{rank} J(p)-1\bigr) \;=\; (n+1) - \operatorname{rank} J(p) \;=\; \dim \ker J(p).$$

### Step 5. The criterion

Combining with the affine criterion, $p\in V$ is nonsingular iff
$$\dim T_p(V\cap U_\ell) \;=\; \dim V \;\iff\; (n+1) - \operatorname{rank} J(p) \;=\; \dim V \;\iff\; \operatorname{rank} J(p) \;=\; n+1-\dim V - 1 \;=\; n - \dim V.$$
Since $n - \dim V = \operatorname{codim}(V)$, we obtain
$$\boxed{\;\operatorname{Sing}(V) \;=\; \bigl\{\,p\in V \;:\; \operatorname{rank}\bigl(\partial F_i/\partial x_j(p)\bigr) \;<\; \operatorname{codim}(V)\,\bigr\}.\;}$$

### Step 6. Consistency check: recovery of the hypersurface case

When $N=1$ and $F=F_1$ is squarefree, $V=V(F)$ is a hypersurface with $\operatorname{codim}(V)=1$. The condition $\operatorname{rank} J(p)<1$ means $\operatorname{rank} J(p)=0$, i.e.
$$\frac{\partial F}{\partial x_0}(p)=\frac{\partial F}{\partial x_1}(p)=\cdots=\frac{\partial F}{\partial x_n}(p)=0,$$
together with $F(p)=0$ (which is $p\in V$). This is exactly the classical characterization of singular points of a projective hypersurface. $\checkmark$

### Step 7. Remarks on reducible $V$ and the generating set

- If $V$ is reducible, $\operatorname{Sing}(V)$ additionally includes the pairwise intersections of distinct irreducible components; the rank condition above captures these as well, since at an intersection point the local dimension jumps and the required rank $\operatorname{codim}(V)$ (computed with $\dim V = \max \dim V_i$) is harder to meet.
- The criterion is independent of the choice of homogeneous generators $F_1,\dots,F_N$ of $I(V)$ up to radical: replacing generators by linear combinations only performs row operations on $J$, preserving rank; adding generators from $I(V)$ adds rows that are $\mathcal{O}$-linear combinations of existing ones at smooth points, so the rank at smooth points is stable at $\operatorname{codim}(V)$.

This completes the characterization.

